        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-2f8802c0eee447aa\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite’s embedded “je t’aime” (“I love you”) straightforwardly avows her loving state and serves as a reason why Armand’s father should accept her. Although the relationship involves sacrifice and plans for a shared future, this clause itself does not undertake or renew a commitment. Nothing presents the words as an affective or reflex-like discharge rather than an emotionally meaningful assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marguerite directly addresses Armand; the love utterance is syntactically embedded in the causal clause “puisque je t’aime.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After learning of Armand’s conflict with his father, Marguerite argues that the father should be pleased she is Armand’s mistress because she loves him and seeks no more than his means permit. The avowal therefore has a reason-giving and reassuring role. Her concern about separating Armand from his family, their arrangements for the future, and her later assurance that she will remain his provide evidence of sincere attachment, but they do not turn this particular clause into a commitment-performing or reflexive utterance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“il devrait être heureux que ce fût moi, puisque je t’aime” (“he ought to be glad that it is I, since I love you”)\",\"supports\":\"The causal construction presents her love as a fact or avowed state supporting an argument, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite adds that she “n’ambitionne pas plus que ta position ne le permet” and asks whether Armand explained how they arranged their future.\",\"supports\":\"This situates the avowal within reassurance about her motives and their relationship, while not making the core clause itself an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Armand says their decision gave his father “la preuve de notre amour mutuel” (“proof of our mutual love”).\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding dialogue treats love as an existing mutual emotional state, reinforcing the truth-conditional reading.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The utterance occurs in a sustained, deliberative discussion of Armand’s father and the couple’s future, without narration that Marguerite blurts or involuntarily produces the words.\",\"supports\":\"The delivery supplies no positive evidence for independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified in Chapter XXI of Alexandre Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the location and grammatical form of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the clause’s principal force as an avowal presented as true. Its argumentative and reassuring contextual roles require no additional core category, and there is no independent evidence for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
